<<~ ahu #/head>>

! The crypto boundary ~ define the need, implement nothing

Every primitive this house relies on arrives from an audited library or from the platform. This module
names WHAT the house needs and hands back one shape for it, so a caller reaches a shore rather than a
runtime.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/law>>

!! The doctrines

# ''Lararium defines needs; it implements no primitive.'' A hand-rolled digest, cipher or random
source belongs to nobody who can audit it.
# ''Portable code depends on THIS module's shores.'' Never on Node's crypto, never on a browser global
reached directly, never on hand-written arithmetic. One import, and the same law in every runtime.
# ''A caller supplies CANONICAL BYTES, never a raw object.'' `canonicalJson` sorts keys and refuses
what has no stable JSON reading — undefined, a function, a symbol, a BigInt, a non-finite number, a
class instance without a well-typed representation. Two callers that serialized differently would
digest the same value to two addresses.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-two-digest-paths>>

!! Two paths, one digest

Both stand platform-blind and both produce full-hex sha256 — the single-cid-gate isomorphism, so a
value addressed through one path answers at the same address through the other.

''The SYNC family rides `@noble/hashes` directly'' — audited, zero-dependency, browser-shippable — and
carries the live runtime digest: the action-handler's render-hash, persona-hd, the genesis doc, and
the memetic-wikitext reader's source cid.

''The ASYNC shore takes a provider'' and serves the caller that INJECTS its own digest — for
auditability, or to route through an HSM or a worker — or that already lives async. Reaching for it
elsewhere buys a promise where a value would do.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-buffer-constraint>>

!! Why the random port names its buffer

`getRandomValues` binds `T extends Uint8Array<ArrayBuffer>` rather than a bare `Uint8Array`.

''Web Crypto REFUSES a view backed by a SharedArrayBuffer.'' A `Uint8Array` carries its backing buffer
as a type parameter, so an unconstrained signature admits a shared view that every implementation
behind this port declines at runtime — ''the port would promise a fill nobody performs.'' Naming the
buffer moves that refusal to the call site, where a caller can still choose differently.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

